tier: opus
branch: feat/prod-install-bootstrap
depends_on:
owns: install.sh, README.md, src/charon/doctor.py, docs/   # PROVISIONAL — finalize at activation
prompt: (authored on activation — trust model + hosting URL RESOLVED 2026-06-27, see DESIGN NOTE)
scope: PRODUCTION-READINESS. A one-liner bootstrap installer + friendly prereq handling so a
  fresh user gets Charon running without hitting cryptic failures. MOTIVATION: on Ubuntu 22.04
  LTS (Python 3.10, no pip) a fresh user CANNOT follow the README `pipx install` line — they hit
  a cryptic `No module named pip` and a too-old Python (Charon requires-python >=3.11). Real
  production-readiness barrier surfaced on charon-vm 2026-06-27. REQUIREMENTS:
  1. ONE-LINER BOOTSTRAP — `curl -fsSL https://<stable-url>/install.sh | bash` (+ a `wget -qO-`
     variant), hosted over HTTPS from a stable GitHub **release asset** (NOT raw master — see
     RESOLVED decision below), repo is public. HARDEN the EXISTING `install.sh`, do not greenfield.
  2. PREREQ CHECK + INSTALL — detect OS/distro + package manager (apt/dnf/brew); check Python
     >=3.11, git, and pip/pipx; install what's missing (escalate to sudo ONLY for the
     package-manager step, with a clear prompt) OR, if it can't, print the EXACT manual commands.
     Replace cryptic failures (e.g. `No module named pip`) with clear "you need X; run Y".
  3. INSTALL CHARON — using the detected/installed Python 3.11 (pipx, or a venv fallback).
  4. FINISH WITH NEXT STEPS / HELP — how to run the gateway, and after setup: (a) how to point an
     app at Charon — the OpenAI-compatible base URL `http://<host>:8080/v1` + the token as the
     API key (token REQUIRED for non-loopback `--host 0.0.0.0`); (b) the webui/console URL
     (`/` or `/charon` on `127.0.0.1:8080`). Link to the relevant help/docs.
  5. SCRIPT HYGIENE — idempotent, re-runnable, short + inspectable, minimal privilege; offer a
     "download -> inspect -> run" path too.
  6. LOW-EFFORT pretty output (approved 2026-06-27) — the installer should look better than raw
     terminal text using PURE BASH only (zero extra dependencies, universal compatibility):
     colored section headers + bold key values, green ✓ / red ✗ status lines per prereq
     checked/installed, step counters like `[2/5] Installing Python 3.11…`, and an optional
     ASCII-safe banner. MUST auto-degrade to plain text when output is not a TTY or `NO_COLOR` is
     set (never garble logs/CI/old terminals). An optional simple pure-bash spinner during long
     downloads is acceptable. The same minimal-ANSI style can optionally extend to `charon setup`
     and gateway startup for a consistent look.
     PARKED / out-of-scope (with rationale): heavier "pretty" approaches are explicitly NOT in
     scope — ncurses TUI installers (`whiptail`/`dialog`) add packages that may not be installed
     (the very compatibility friction we're avoiding), and Python `rich`/`textual` need Python
     already present (chicken-and-egg for a bootstrapper) and add a dependency (Charon core is
     deliberately stdlib-only). Revisit only if there's a strong reason.
  7. LAYER-2 FOLLOW-ON (post-install, sub-item) — extend `charon doctor` toward a fuller
     preflight/environment check (config present, gateway reachable on `127.0.0.1:8080` via
     `GET /v1/models`, models configured). DISTINCT from the pre-install bootstrap. Note: `charon
     doctor` currently probes an ACP agent, not the gateway — this redirects/augments it toward
     the gateway-first product surface.
  8. UPDATE / REINSTALL path (approved 2026-06-27) — Charon currently has NO built-in
     update/upgrade/reinstall command; updating means remembering pip/pipx incantations (e.g.
     `pipx reinstall charon` or `pip install --upgrade --force-reinstall "git+…"`). New users
     conflate this with `reset`/`setup`, which only touch *config*, not the *program*.
     - The one-liner installer must be **RE-RUNNABLE to update**: running it again detects an
       existing install, pulls the newest version, and reinstalls cleanly (idempotent). This is
       the primary update mechanism.
     - It MUST **preserve user config** (`~/.charon` — providers/keys/models/pools) across an
       update/reinstall; never silently wipe settings. Offer an explicit `--reinstall` /
       clean-slate flag for users who DO want a fresh config.
     - Add a thin **`charon update`** (and/or `charon reinstall`) subcommand that shells out to
       the right pip/pipx command so users don't have to remember it. DESIGN NOTE: an in-app
       self-update that replaces the running interpreter/binary is fiddly (self-replacement) — so
       the safe MVP is the re-runnable installer + a `charon update` that just prints/runs the
       correct `pipx reinstall` / `pip install --upgrade` command for how it was installed;
       capture the self-replacement caveat.
     - Clarify the **program-vs-settings distinction** in help/docs: `update`/`reinstall` = the
       program; `setup`/`reset` = your settings. (This confusion is itself why the command + clear
       docs are needed.)
  CONSTRAINTS: product-clean (no SLOP/fleet/rig leak), agent/provider-agnostic.
  DESIGN NOTE (RESOLVED — operator decided 2026-06-27):
  - **Decision 1 (trust model): offer BOTH.** Ship the one-liner `curl -fsSL …/install.sh | bash`
    (+ the `wget -qO-` variant) for convenience, AND document a "download → inspect → run" path
    for cautious users. The bootstrap stays inspectable, idempotent, and minimal-privilege.
  - **Decision 2 (hosting): Option 2 — GitHub release asset.** Host `install.sh` from a stable
    GitHub **release asset** over HTTPS, NOT raw master, so users get a vetted/pinned installer
    rather than whatever is on the default branch. Keep the script short + inspectable +
    idempotent.
  - **Cost note:** hosting via GitHub public-repo release assets + source clone is **free at
    scale** (prereqs come from the user's OS mirrors, not us); PyPI publishing is the natural
    future scale upgrade, not required now.
note: PARKED. PRODUCTION-READINESS PRIORITY (strong candidate to build soon — directly serves the
  fresh-install-just-works north-star). Surfaced on charon-vm 2026-06-27 (Ubuntu 22.04 / Python
  3.10 / no pip). Trust model + hosting URL RESOLVED 2026-06-27 (offer BOTH; GitHub release
  asset) — ready to author the prompt and activate.

